(Determining g with a simple pendulum) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Research question) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(How does the length of a pendulum affect it?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Background) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A simple pendulum is a mass on a string that swings back and forth. Galileo studied) Tj T*
(pendulums and noticed that they keep good time. For small angles the period is given by T) Tj T*
(= 2pisqrt\(L/g\), where T is the period, L is the length and g is the acceleration due to) Tj T*
(gravity. Pendulums are used in clocks and in many other places. Squaring both sides gives) Tj T*
(T² = \(4pi²/g\)L, so a graph of T² against L should be a straight line through the origin.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I chose this topic because my grandfather, Jordan Avery senior, repaired clocks and I) Tj T*
(wanted to understand how they work.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Variables) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Independent variable: length of the string.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Dependent variable: period.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Control variables: mass of the bob, angle of release, the same stopwatch.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Method) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Apparatus: a clamp stand, string, a 50 g mass, a metre rule \(resolution 1 mm\) and a) Tj T*
(stopwatch \(resolution 0.01 s\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. Tie the string to the clamp and attach the mass.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. Measure the length.) Tj T*
